      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Can You Apply for BC PNP Without a Job Offer? (2026 Answer + Alternatives)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        The short answer is no for workers, and the old exceptions are gone. Here is what each BC PNP stream requires, where the LMIA fits in, and the options that are actually open to you without a B.C. job offer.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-18">October 18, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>13 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-indigo-50 border-l-4 border-indigo-600 p-6 rounded-r-xl mb-10">
      <p class="font-bold text-indigo-900 mb-2">Quick answer</p>
      <p class="text-indigo-900">
        No. As of October 2026 you cannot apply for BC PNP without a job offer as a skilled worker. Both remaining Skills Immigration streams, Skilled Worker and Health Authority (with or without the Express Entry BC option), require a full-time, indeterminate job offer from an eligible B.C. employer. The only BC PNP route with no job offer is Entrepreneur Immigration, where you invest in and run your own B.C. business. An LMIA is not required.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">0</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">BC PNP worker streams open without a job offer</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">2</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Entrepreneur streams (Base, Regional) with no job offer</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">Jan 2025</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">When the last no-job-offer worker stream closed</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        "Can I apply for BC PNP without a job offer?" used to have the answer "sometimes": some B.C. master's and PhD graduates could apply without one through the International Post-Graduate stream. That door closed in January 2025, and in April 2026 the BC Provincial Nominee Program confirmed it will not launch new student streams. Today every BC PNP worker pathway starts with a genuine offer from an eligible B.C. employer. This guide sets out what each stream requires, clears up the LMIA confusion and lists alternatives you can verify on official pages. It is general information, not legal advice.
      </p>

      <nav class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-10 not-prose" aria-label="Table of contents">
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">On this page</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1 text-slate-700">
          <li><a href="#streams" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Does BC PNP require a job offer? Stream by stream</a></li>
          <li><a href="#old-exceptions" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">The old no-job-offer stream and why it is gone</a></li>
          <li><a href="#entrepreneur" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">The one exception: Entrepreneur Immigration</a></li>
          <li><a href="#valid-offer" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">What counts as a valid BC PNP job offer</a></li>
          <li><a href="#lmia" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Do you need an LMIA for BC PNP?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#options" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Your real options without a B.C. job offer</a></li>
          <li><a href="#examples" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Two worked examples</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mistakes" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Common mistakes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="streams" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Does BC PNP Require a Job Offer? Stream by Stream</h2>

      <p>
        Yes, for every worker stream. Since the April 23, 2026 restructuring around the Care, Build and Innovate objectives, the Skills Immigration Program Guide lists two streams, Skilled Worker and Health Authority, plus a temporary initiative for rural health authority cleaning and security staff. Each one is built on a job offer. The Express Entry BC (EEBC) option is not a separate stream; it is a choice you make inside one of those two streams if you also qualify for a federal Express Entry program.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">BC PNP stream</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Status (October 2026)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Job offer needed?</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Skilled Worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes: full-time, indeterminate, NOC TEER 0–3, eligible B.C. employer, plus two years of skilled experience</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Health Authority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes: from a B.C. public health authority, or health authority support for certain physicians, nurse practitioners and midwives</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Express Entry BC option (of either stream)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes: same job offer as the stream, plus a valid federal Express Entry profile</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Temporary Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Time-limited</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes: health authority employees in rural or remote areas in NOC 64410, 65310 or 65312</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Entry Level and Semi-Skilled (ELSS)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Closed (announced April 23, 2026; no invitations since December 10, 2024)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Required one, plus nine months with the employer</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">International Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Closed (November 2024)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Required one</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">International Post-Graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Closed (January 2025)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No: this was the last no-job-offer worker stream</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Planned Bachelor's, Master's and Doctorate streams</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Never launched; BC said on April 23, 2026 it will not launch new student streams</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not applicable</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Entrepreneur Immigration: Base</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open, with regular invitation rounds in 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No, but you must invest in and actively manage a B.C. business</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Entrepreneur Immigration: Regional</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open, with smaller invitation rounds in 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No, but you need a community referral and a business in a participating community</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The priority technology draws also ended. Tech workers can still be invited through High Economic Impact rounds, but only with a qualifying B.C. job offer. Our guide for <a href="/articles/tech-workers-bc-pnp-2027-after-tech-draws-ended" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">tech workers after the tech draws ended</a> explains those rounds.
      </p>

      <h2 id="old-exceptions" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Old No-Job-Offer Stream, and Why It Is Gone</h2>

      <p>
        Much online advice still says a B.C. master's or PhD lets you skip the job offer. That described the <strong>International Post-Graduate (IPG)</strong> stream, for graduates of B.C. institutions in eligible natural, applied and health sciences fields. It was popular: the BC PNP's 2025 statistical report shows International Graduate and IPG files made up about half of 2025 Skills Immigration nominations, mostly from applications already in the inventory.
      </p>
      <p>
        The International Graduate stream (which did require a job offer) closed on November 26, 2024, and the IPG stream closed on January 7, 2025, according to contemporaneous reporting of the BC PNP's notices. The province planned new Bachelor's, Master's and Doctorate streams, then suspended them in April 2025 "until B.C.'s allocation levels are restored." On April 23, 2026 it ended the question: "The BC PNP will not be launching new student streams." It added that completing studies in B.C. or Canada will continue to earn registration points. Our <a href="/articles/international-graduates-bc-pr-options-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">guide for international graduates</a> covers what those points are worth.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Watch for outdated pages</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">If a website, agent or video tells you a B.C. "PhD stream" or "Master's stream" lets you skip the job offer in 2026, it is out of date or wrong. Check the current Skills Immigration Program Guide on WelcomeBC, which lists only the Skilled Worker and Health Authority streams.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="entrepreneur" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The One Exception: BC PNP Entrepreneur Immigration</h2>

      <p>
        If you are an experienced business owner or senior manager, you can apply to the BC PNP without a job offer through Entrepreneur Immigration. There is no employer, because the plan is that you create your own job, and at least one more for a Canadian citizen or permanent resident. It is a demanding route:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Base stream:</strong> a business anywhere in B.C., with a minimum personal net worth of $600,000 and an eligible personal investment of at least $200,000.</li>
        <li><strong>Regional stream:</strong> lower thresholds ($300,000 net worth and $100,000 investment) for a business in a participating smaller community, with a mandatory exploratory visit and a referral from that community.</li>
        <li><strong>How it works:</strong> you register a business proposal, compete in invitation rounds, have your net worth verified by an approved firm, sign a performance agreement and come to B.C. on a work permit. You are nominated for permanent residence only after you have implemented the business and filed a final report.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Invitation rounds continued through 2026 but are small: the September 22, 2026 round invited 10 Base candidates and fewer than five Regional candidates, with a minimum score of 119 in both. This route suits someone who wants to run a business, not someone looking for a job. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-entrepreneur-immigration" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Entrepreneur Immigration guide</a> covers the steps and costs.
      </p>

      <h2 id="valid-offer" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Counts as a Valid BC PNP Job Offer</h2>

      <p>
        A "job offer" for the BC PNP is a specific legal requirement, not just a letter from an employer who likes you. Based on the current Skills Immigration Program Guide, the offer and the employer must meet all of these conditions:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Full-time and indeterminate.</strong> No end date. Casual, part-time, seasonal and fixed-term jobs do not qualify.</li>
        <li><strong>An eligible occupation.</strong> The Skilled Worker stream needs NOC TEER 0–3. For applications submitted after June 13, 2026, 12 occupations cannot be nominated in any stream, including administrative assistants, bookkeepers, HR officers, retail and food service supervisors and real estate agents. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-ineligible-occupations-employers-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">ineligible occupations guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>A fair wage.</strong> Within the WorkBC or Job Bank range for the occupation and location, and in line with what the employer pays Canadians in similar roles.</li>
        <li><strong>An established B.C. employer.</strong> At least one year operating in B.C., a fixed place of business, and at least five full-time employees in Metro Vancouver or three elsewhere. Staffing agencies and immigration-services businesses generally cannot support you.</li>
        <li><strong>Genuine recruitment.</strong> The employer normally advertises locally first; this may be considered met if you already work full-time for it in the same occupation.</li>
        <li><strong>No family ownership</strong> of 10% or more of the employer, and you must hold any licence or certification the job needs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-requirements-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP job offer requirements guide</a> goes through each condition and the employer paperwork in more detail.
      </p>

      <h2 id="lmia" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Do You Need an LMIA for BC PNP?</h2>

      <p>
        No. A Labour Market Impact Assessment (LMIA) is a federal document an employer gets from Employment and Social Development Canada (ESDC) so it can hire a foreign worker on a work permit. Whether you ask "does BC PNP need LMIA" or "is LMIA required for BC PNP," the answer is the same: the province applies its own job offer rules, and an LMIA is neither required nor worth extra registration points.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where an LMIA does matter is your <em>work permit</em>. If you are in B.C. on a post-graduation work permit or a spousal open work permit, you can work for your supporting employer without one. If you are outside Canada and want to start the job before you are nominated, your employer may need an LMIA so you can get an employer-specific work permit, and ESDC requires the employer, not you, to pay the $1,000 fee. After nomination, the BC PNP may issue a work permit support letter so you can apply for a work permit while IRCC processes your permanent residence. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-lmia-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP and LMIA guide</a> covers the work permit side in depth.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">New federal change on November 3, 2026</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">IRCC announced on October 16, 2026 that, from November 3, an LMIA will no longer be required for a qualifying job offer under Express Entry, either for Comprehensive Ranking System (CRS) points or for the job offer rules of the Federal Skilled Worker and Federal Skilled Trades programs. IRCC's changes page says job offer points will go only to jobs in high-wage occupations, those with a national median wage at least 1.3 times the workforce average, worth 50, 70 or 100 points by wage tier. Profiles in the pool will be rescored automatically, and invitation rounds may pause for up to two weeks. This is a federal change. It does not change BC PNP rules, which never required an LMIA.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="options" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Apply for BC PNP Without a Job Offer: Your Real Options</h2>

      <p>
        Because the direct route is closed, the useful question is how to get to permanent residence, or to a B.C. job offer, from where you are now. These are the four realistic paths.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Option 1: Qualify for Express Entry on your own</h3>
      <p>
        Federal Express Entry does not require a job offer for the Canadian Experience Class (CEC) or the Federal Skilled Worker Program (FSW). IRCC's minimum requirements:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>CEC:</strong> at least one year (1,560 hours) of paid, skilled Canadian work experience in TEER 0–3 within the last three years, gained while authorized to work. Work during full-time study, co-op terms and self-employment do not count. No education requirement and no proof of funds.</li>
        <li><strong>FSW:</strong> at least one year of continuous paid skilled work experience within the last 10 years, CLB 7 in all four abilities, a Canadian credential or a foreign one with an Educational Credential Assessment, and at least 67 of 100 points on the FSW grid. You need proof of settlement funds unless you are authorized to work in Canada and hold a valid job offer; IRCC updates the amounts each year.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Eligibility is not the same as an invitation. In IRCC's 2026 rounds up to October 16, CEC rounds had cutoffs between 507 and 523, French-language rounds between 382 and 435, healthcare and social services rounds between 467 and 475, and trades rounds 476 to 477. Category rounds require at least 12 months of experience in an eligible occupation within the past three years. We found no general or FSW-only rounds in 2026, which makes Express Entry very hard for candidates abroad with no Canadian experience, French or category occupation. The November 3 changes add points for Canadian experience in high-wage occupations, which favours people already working here. For the full federal picture, see our guide to <a href="/articles/how-to-become-permanent-resident-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how to become a permanent resident of Canada</a> and our analysis of <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry category changes</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Option 2: Other provinces with no-job-offer pathways</h3>
      <p>
        A few provinces still have pathways that do not need a job offer, but in 2026 most of them are closed in practice, rarely used or tied to an existing connection to the province. We list only what we confirmed on each province's official website in October 2026.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Province and pathway</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Job offer?</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Catch</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Intake status, October 2026</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Saskatchewan: International Skilled Worker – Occupation In-Demand</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">60 of 110 points, CLB 4, related post-secondary education, one year of skilled experience in an in-demand occupation within 10 years</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Expressions of interest accepted, but the SINP says "there are no scheduled EOI draws at this time"; its results document lists September 12, 2024 as the latest selection</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Saskatchewan: International Skilled Worker – Saskatchewan Express Entry</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Same 60-point grid plus a federal Express Entry profile</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Same as above: no scheduled draws</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Alberta: Express Entry Stream – priority sector draws</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not always required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CRS of at least 300, an occupation in a priority sector such as construction, manufacturing, agriculture, aviation or health care, a Worker EOI and intent to live in Alberta; AAIP does not disclose its draw parameters</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Draws ran through 2026, but only 3 of 619 priority sector spaces were left as of October 7, 2026</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Manitoba: Skilled Worker Overseas</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You need an established connection: family or friends in Manitoba, past study or work there, or a direct invitation through a strategic recruitment initiative</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The October 8, 2026 draw considered Skilled Worker candidates only if directly invited by the MPNP or covered by a federal temporary public policy</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Nova Scotia: Express Entry</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">At least one year of TEER 0–3 work experience in Nova Scotia, so it does not help people outside the province</td><td class="px-4 py-3">EOI system with selections by priority; fees apply from September 1, 2026</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ontario: Human Capital Priorities</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Was not required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Closed with all other old OINP streams on June 25, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Closed; the new Ontario Workforce Priority stream needs a job offer, except for self-employed physicians</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        We could not confirm current rules for New Brunswick or Prince Edward Island on their official sites, so we have not listed them; check gnb.ca and princeedwardisland.ca directly. Every PNP requires a genuine intention to live in the nominating province, so a nomination elsewhere is not a back door to B.C. For province-by-province context see <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">best provinces for PNP in 2027</a>, <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-saskatchewan-sinp-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC vs Saskatchewan</a> and <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-alberta-aaip-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC vs Alberta</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Option 3: Get a genuine B.C. job offer</h3>
      <p>
        For most people who want to live in B.C., this is the real path. A few practical points:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Search where B.C. employers post.</strong> The <a href="https://www.workbc.ca/search-and-prepare-job/find-jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">WorkBC Job Board</a> and the federal Job Bank list B.C. vacancies. Health workers can use <a href="https://www.bchealthcareers.ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC Health Careers</a>, which the program guide notes recruits for the health authorities free of charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Aim where BC PNP is inviting.</strong> Care occupations and the nine Build trades have had much lower cutoffs than other jobs. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-care-healthcare-occupations-list-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Care occupations list</a> and <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-build-skilledtradesbc-certification-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Build trades guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Look outside Metro Vancouver.</strong> BC PNP expects at least 35% of nominations to go to workers outside Metro Vancouver, and regional jobs earn extra registration points.</li>
        <li><strong>Never pay for a job.</strong> The program guide states that in B.C. "it is illegal to buy jobs, and it is illegal for anyone to ask you for money or any other compensation in exchange for a job." Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-scams-fraud-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">job offer scams guide</a> lists the red flags and where to report them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Once you have an offer, run it through the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> to see how your score compares with recent cutoffs before you register.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Option 4: Work in B.C. first on another permit</h3>
      <p>
        Many BC PNP nominees did not start with a permanent job offer. They came on a temporary work permit, proved themselves to an employer, and then asked that employer for an indeterminate offer. Common starting points include a post-graduation work permit after eligible studies, an International Experience Canada working holiday permit for eligible young people, a spousal open work permit (eligibility was narrowed in 2025, so check IRCC's current rules), an intra-company transfer, or an employer-specific permit supported by an LMIA.
      </p>
      <p>
        Working in B.C. first earns BC PNP points for Canadian experience and for currently working for your supporting employer, may satisfy the local recruitment requirement, and after one year of skilled work makes you eligible for the CEC. The risk is time: federal policy aims to bring temporary residents below 5% of the population by the end of 2027, so permits are harder to get and extend. Read our guide to the <a href="/articles/canada-temporary-resident-cap-2027-bc-work-permit-holders" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">temporary resident cap and B.C. work permit holders</a> before you plan around one, and our guide to <a href="/articles/how-to-immigrate-to-canada-with-family" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">immigrating with family</a> if a spouse or children are coming too.
      </p>

      <h2 id="examples" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Two Worked Examples</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">A software developer abroad with no Canadian ties</h3>
      <p>
        Hypothetical: master's degree, four years as a developer overseas, CLB 9 English, no French, no job offer. She cannot register with the BC PNP. She meets the FSW minimums, but with no general, FSW-only or STEM rounds in 2026, eligibility alone is unlikely to bring an invitation. Saskatchewan has no scheduled draws, and Alberta's tech pathway needs an Alberta job offer. Her best moves are French toward NCLC 7, or a genuine B.C. offer at a wage that clears High Economic Impact thresholds ($52 an hour and $105,000 a year in the September 24, 2026 round).
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">A registered nurse overseas</h3>
      <p>
        Hypothetical: five years as a registered nurse abroad, CLB 8. Her path to B.C. runs through a job offer, but a reachable one. If she registers with the BC College of Nurses and Midwives and gets a regular, full-time, indeterminate position with a health authority, she can apply under the Health Authority stream without waiting for a draw. With a private employer instead, she would compete in Care: Health rounds, which had cutoffs of 76 in September and October 2026. Nova Scotia, which lists healthcare as a top priority for international applicants, is a reasonable second option.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mistakes" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Relying on pre-2025 advice.</strong> The IPG stream, the graduate streams and the separate tech draws are gone.</li>
        <li><strong>Thinking an LMIA is the problem.</strong> BC PNP needs a job offer, not an LMIA. An LMIA only matters for your work permit.</li>
        <li><strong>Accepting a job that can never be nominated.</strong> Check the 12 ineligible occupations and the employer rules before you sign.</li>
        <li><strong>Registering with an inflated offer.</strong> Overstated duties or wages can lead to refusal and a ban of up to two years.</li>
        <li><strong>Paying an "agent" for an offer.</strong> It is illegal in B.C., and you carry the consequences.</li>
      </ul>

